dsv41 patch 0079 (22.10.2026): decode fixed costs (global top-k reuse, shared-expert zeros). In the image, off.

Base: image v23 (flashinfer_sparse.py, moe_method.py = gh_stage v22; neither touched by 0075-0078). All switches default
off = v23 bit for bit. Both levers are exact (the same values reach the same kernels).

What
  0079a VLLM_DSV41_GTOPK_REUSE=1: decode calls compute_global_topk_indices_and_lens once a layer (38 a round, profile
        P_D0: 4.0 us each). Layers sharing index source, KV source (block table) and block size get the same result:
        the first of each group computes it and stores it in the step's metadata dict flashinfer_sparse_index_cache
        (fresh per build(), cleared on draft updates, the dict upstream already uses for SWA-only layers); the rest
        reuse the tensors (read only by FlashInfer / split_sparse_mla / the Triton verify path). 8 launches a round.
  0079c VLLM_EXL3_MOE_SHARED_ZEROS=1: with the shared expert inside exl3_smoe, _apply_with_shared gives vLLM's runner a
        zero shared output (the runner adds shared + routed). Today torch.zeros_like = one fill kernel a MoE layer
        (FillFunctor<bf16>, 15 blocks, 1.5 us, 40 a round); on: a view of one persistent zero buffer (64 rows, allocated
        outside graph capture, never written). The runner's add stays, so the sum is bit-identical.
Why
  Kernel census P_D0_n1 (loops/analyse): 70 of the ~1,400 launches a decode round are these two; raw profile shows the
  fill + add pair after every smoe_down and the global-index kernel before every sparse-MLA decode call.
Not built (and why)
  D1 lm-head / drafter head on exl3_mrow at 9-24 rows: configuration only (EXL3_HEAD_MROW_MAX_ROWS), needs the
     serve-off microbench first (fenster14/mb_dense_rows.py, MB_FORCE_MROW32=1, EXL3_MROW32_VARIANT=ring): head.py's
     own numbers (before the ring kernel) had mrow slower than exl3 at 12-16 rows.
  D2 ring sum folded into the 0048 mHC boundary: CUDA in exl3_rdma/exl3_mhc plus the two-rank protocol; no offline test.
  Query padding (PLAN's "glue"): with 32 local heads FlashInfer needs no padding (_pad_to_supported_q_heads(32) = 32);
     the fp8->bf16 cast per layer is needed by the SM120 kernel. The _qdq_ue8m0 fold and the o_proj copy would change
     Triton/CUDA kernels of the 0032 parity path.
Expected effect
  ~70 launches less a round: ~0.18 ms (0.6 % at 1 stream, ~0.35 % at 4) - at the noise edge; measure together.
Measured quantities
  decode_ab cells 1/2/4 streams (ms/round geo), t0_ident 8/8, nsys census: compute_global_topk 38 -> 8, FillFunctor 40 -> 0.
Test: v0079/test/test_0079.py (CPU, Mac): real _forward_decode / _apply_with_shared by AST extraction.

Measured (22.10.2026, image v24): T=0 8/8 identical; decode -0.32 % (geometric mean of 12 cells, -4.6..+2.9 %)
against the same configuration without it, inside the run-to-run noise (+-0.6 %). The estimated gain of 0.18 ms per
round (0.35-0.6 %) is not measurable; not adopted.

